WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.360
Hello.

00:01.360 --> 00:03.700
My name is Eunggyu.

00:03.700 --> 00:05.030
I'm the person who built Nawana.

00:05.030 --> 00:06.970
Let me start with why I made it.

00:06.970 --> 00:12.040
That story explains almost everything about how this app works.

00:12.040 --> 00:13.320
I studied in Korea.

00:13.320 --> 00:15.330
In my second year of university,

00:15.330 --> 00:17.740
it hit me that I really needed English.

00:17.740 --> 00:21.710
So I transferred to a university in Newcastle, in England.

00:21.710 --> 00:24.310
But my English at that point was bad.

00:24.310 --> 00:25.800
Really bad.

00:25.800 --> 00:27.150
So before term started,

00:27.150 --> 00:30.660
I spent six months in Portsmouth doing an IELTS course.

00:30.660 --> 00:32.000
It was a good start.

00:32.000 --> 00:34.060
University was still hard.

00:34.060 --> 00:37.600
Anyway, I graduated, found a job in Germany,

00:37.600 --> 00:41.110
and I've been living here almost sixteen years now.

00:41.110 --> 00:43.160
Here's the part that matters.

00:43.160 --> 00:45.520
Almost all of my work has been in English.

00:45.520 --> 00:48.510
Client meetings, projects, managing teams —

00:48.510 --> 00:49.890
all of it in English.

00:49.890 --> 00:52.280
And I have never once felt fluent.

00:52.280 --> 00:54.490
I could get through the situation.

00:54.490 --> 00:57.180
I just couldn't say what I wanted to say,

00:57.180 --> 00:59.210
the way I wanted to say it.

00:59.210 --> 01:01.210
That gap never closed.

01:01.210 --> 01:03.160
So first — I built this app,

01:03.160 --> 01:05.570
but I'm not someone who has solved English,

01:05.570 --> 01:08.680
this homework that never ends for us.

01:08.680 --> 01:10.600
I'm practicing with it myself right now.

01:11.150 --> 01:15.640
So let me tell you what makes Nawana different from other English learning apps,

01:15.640 --> 01:18.210
and what problem it's trying to solve.

01:18.210 --> 01:19.570
When you start the app,

01:19.570 --> 01:22.390
the first thing you'll do is let it hear your voice.

01:22.390 --> 01:23.610
It takes one minute.

01:23.610 --> 01:27.200
And from that voice, the app builds your future self.

01:27.200 --> 01:28.200
That's right.

01:28.200 --> 01:31.090
Using AI, it creates the future you —

01:31.090 --> 01:33.920
the one who speaks English fluently.

01:33.920 --> 01:35.610
That's the heart of this app.

01:35.610 --> 01:37.330
Let me explain why.

01:37.330 --> 01:39.710
When we listen to a native speaker — in a video,

01:39.710 --> 01:40.640
in a classroom —

01:40.640 --> 01:44.910
some part of our brain files it away like this: that's them,

01:44.910 --> 01:45.690
not me.

01:45.690 --> 01:50.720
A perfect accent coming out of someone else's mouth actually feels like a wall to us.

01:50.720 --> 01:54.210
But when it's your own voice, it's different.

01:54.210 --> 01:58.070
Your tone, your timbre, the sound you actually make —

01:58.070 --> 02:00.130
coming back at you fluent.

02:00.130 --> 02:01.760
That wall disappears.

02:01.760 --> 02:05.780
It stops feeling like something to admire from a distance.

02:05.780 --> 02:06.970
It feels close.

02:06.970 --> 02:08.250
Not a different world —

02:08.250 --> 02:11.000
more like somewhere you could actually reach.

02:11.000 --> 02:12.410
That's how it feels.

02:12.410 --> 02:14.550
And that becomes our real goal.

02:14.550 --> 02:18.090
Not imitating the sound of someone else's pronunciation.

02:18.090 --> 02:21.520
That's why building your future voice is the first step.

02:21.520 --> 02:25.090
Once your future self exists, there are two ways to use it.

02:25.090 --> 02:26.470
The first is Talk.

02:26.470 --> 02:29.750
You speak, and your future self answers.

02:29.750 --> 02:31.820
You can just talk freely.

02:31.820 --> 02:36.090
No topic, no script — start, and follow wherever it goes.

02:36.090 --> 02:38.240
Or pick a news topic.

02:38.240 --> 02:40.390
When you build your profile at the beginning,

02:40.390 --> 02:43.710
we ask about your interests, and topics get built from that.

02:43.710 --> 02:47.330
So you're practicing on things you genuinely care about —

02:47.330 --> 02:48.740
with the latest news.

02:48.740 --> 02:52.470
You learn English and pick up some knowledge along the way.

02:52.470 --> 02:53.870
There are scenarios too.

02:53.870 --> 02:57.930
Ready-made ones — ordering at a café, checking into a hotel,

02:57.930 --> 03:00.490
going through airport immigration,

03:00.490 --> 03:02.910
meeting someone for the first time.

03:02.910 --> 03:04.730
Or you can build your own.

03:04.730 --> 03:07.910
If you have an interview coming up, put in the company name,

03:07.910 --> 03:10.690
the position, as much detail as you want.

03:10.690 --> 03:13.050
That becomes your practice.

03:13.050 --> 03:15.720
The second is Watch — and personally,

03:15.720 --> 03:17.650
this is my favorite feature.

03:17.650 --> 03:19.740
First you create a persona.

03:19.740 --> 03:22.580
Someone you might realistically end up talking to.

03:22.580 --> 03:26.780
If there's someone around you that you already speak English with,

03:26.780 --> 03:29.090
set them up as your conversation partner.

03:29.090 --> 03:32.380
Their name, their relationship to you, and so on —

03:32.380 --> 03:35.900
describe the person you know in as much detail as you can.

03:35.900 --> 03:38.690
And if there's no real person around you,

03:38.690 --> 03:41.520
an imaginary one works just as well.

03:41.520 --> 03:42.930
That's up to you.

03:42.930 --> 03:47.830
Then your future self and that person have a conversation about the topic you chose,

03:47.830 --> 03:49.530
and you watch it happen.

03:49.530 --> 03:52.200
If there's a good expression in there, you can shadow it.

03:52.200 --> 03:56.630
The words that come up in the conversation are there for you to study separately.

03:56.630 --> 03:58.150
I think for most learners,

03:58.150 --> 04:00.860
the hard part in conversation isn't grammar.

04:00.860 --> 04:02.680
It's not knowing what to say.

04:02.680 --> 04:04.360
There's no idea there.

04:04.360 --> 04:07.660
And because you've never actually been in these situations yourself,

04:07.660 --> 04:10.330
you don't know whether what you're saying is right,

04:10.330 --> 04:12.960
whether you're even allowed to say it that way.

04:12.960 --> 04:17.650
There's no model for that moment in your head — so you freeze.

04:17.650 --> 04:20.460
Watch simulates that situation for you.

04:20.460 --> 04:22.820
You see how your future self works through it,

04:22.820 --> 04:26.740
which words they use, which expressions sound natural.

04:26.740 --> 04:28.690
And once you've practiced enough,

04:28.690 --> 04:31.940
you run the same situation yourself in Talk.

04:31.940 --> 04:33.690
Watch to get the idea.

04:33.690 --> 04:35.490
Talk to make it yours.

04:35.490 --> 04:37.380
There's more in there too —

04:37.380 --> 04:39.210
things I personally felt were missing.

04:39.210 --> 04:42.710
The shadowing and vocabulary features especially.

04:42.710 --> 04:45.090
I really wanted to get those right.

04:45.090 --> 04:48.790
I'd love to keep going, but that's enough about the app.

04:48.790 --> 04:49.920
Now, a favor to ask.

04:50.350 --> 04:52.330
If you've heard all this and you're interested,

04:52.330 --> 04:54.540
if you want to try it — first,

04:54.540 --> 04:57.290
creating your voice is the most important thing.

04:57.290 --> 04:59.820
Recording quality really matters here.

04:59.820 --> 05:05.890
Because you have to hear yourself speaking fluently before you can feel what this app is worth.

05:05.890 --> 05:08.650
A good microphone would be wonderful,

05:08.650 --> 05:11.180
but your phone is completely fine.

05:11.180 --> 05:14.070
One rule: never use Bluetooth earbuds.

05:14.070 --> 05:18.160
Just put your phone inside your closet, in among the clothes.

05:18.160 --> 05:22.080
And then — this sounds strange — put your head in there too,

05:22.080 --> 05:24.630
and follow what the app tells you.

05:24.630 --> 05:26.310
Under a blanket works as well.

05:26.310 --> 05:28.870
But what matters is being comfortable,

05:28.870 --> 05:31.430
speaking the way you normally speak.

05:31.430 --> 05:32.470
Naturally.

05:32.470 --> 05:35.070
After that, just use it however you like.

05:35.070 --> 05:36.410
And tell me honestly.

05:36.410 --> 05:37.990
Tell me what you liked.

05:37.990 --> 05:40.930
If you've tried other apps, tell me what's lacking here,

05:40.930 --> 05:42.200
what needs improving.

05:42.200 --> 05:46.110
And if you stop using it — tell me where you closed the app.

05:46.110 --> 05:48.110
I'm not looking for encouragement.

05:48.110 --> 05:51.380
I want to know how real users actually use this,

05:51.380 --> 05:53.500
and how it actually feels.

05:53.500 --> 05:55.150
Thank you for starting this with me.

05:55.150 --> 05:56.510
I really hope it helps.
